# The tick contract (excerpt of the bed contract)

### 3.1 Inputs and outputs

Every call gets its input as `task.json` plus read-only files in a fresh per-call workdir, and returns one JSON output
(the stream.mjs convention [M]).

```text
CaseTickInput = { kind: 'case', item, world: <world public id>, instance: 'i-000123', now,
                  events: [Event], carry: <the instance's last committed carry, or null> }
SweepTickInput = { kind: 'sweep', item, world, sweep: <sweep id>, now,
                   index: [{ instance, subject: <arm-declared or null>, openedAt, lastTickAt }],
                   events: [Event],                       // world-level events (list updates, statements, …)
                   carry: <the sweep's last committed carry, or null> }
files: sor/<instance>.json (the business records the instance has declared or touched; §3.4),
       master/<table>.json, rulebook/<edition>/<table>.csv|json (only editions whose file exists in the world at now),
       calendar/<id>.json, docs/<eventId>.txt, api/<system>.schema.json, spec/SPEC-E.md
query: sor.query(table, { where: { field: value | { gte, lte, in } }, limit <= 1000 })
       read-only, deterministic order by primary key, business records only (Track C adds product-computed fields)

TickOutput = {
  decisions:   { subject: <business key or null>, ...item-typed record },     // required on every case-tick
  effects:     [{ key?, system, op, args, compensates?: key, after?: key }],  // applied in order (§3.3)
  obligations: { upsert: [{ id, kind, anchor, dueAt, calendar?, onDue }], cancel: [id] },
  humanTasks:  [{ id, kind, role, excludeUsers?: [userId], subject, question, answerSchema, dueAt, onTimeout }],
  merge?:      { into: <instance> },                  // only on an instance started by an inbound event (§3.2)
  close?:      true,                                  // the instance is done; later deliveries start nothing
  carry }
SweepOutput = { effects: [... each with a subject argument ...], wake: [instance], carry }
```

- **Validation.** The host validates every output against the item's schemas with the W-bed contract validator
  (`beds/wbed/lib/contracts.mjs`, which uses `beds/records/score.mjs` `validate` [M]). An invalid output, a carry above
  64 KB or not JSON, a crash or a timeout is an **arm error** (§3.7).
- **Size.** A case-tick renders to 2–20K tokens inline; master data, tables and documents are files; every prompt stays
  under the 100K-token price step of `policy/models.json` or is priced at the higher rate and logged (E-BED §2.1).
- **Workspace.** An arm that declares one (agent-H-ws) gets a persistent per-arm directory ≤ 50 MB, journaled as
  content-addressed snapshots after every call; not graded.

### 3.2 Events and routing **[D-1]**

```text
Event = { id, type, at, source, channel: { kind: 'instance', instance } | { kind: 'inbound' } | { kind: 'world' },
          payload, injected?: true }
types: migration | doc.received | message.received | effect.result | timer.fired |
       humanTask.answered | humanTask.answerRejected | humanTask.timeout | humanTask.withdrawn | humanTask.delegated |
       edition.published (default variants only) | world.* (sweep-level: list update, statement run, …)
```

The host routes by **channel identity only**, never by business meaning (E-BED §2.2):
- `instance` channel: results of that instance's effects, its timers, answers to its human tasks, and replies that
  quote verbatim a reference the arm itself placed in an outbound message (`args.ref`). Delivered to that instance.
- `inbound` channel: every other inbound document or message (a new invoice, an acquirer response carrying only an
  ARN, a monitoring alert). Each one **starts a new instance** (a message start event) whose first case-tick sees it.
  If the arm decides it belongs to an existing case, it returns `merge: { into }`; the host closes the new instance
  and re-delivers the event to the target in the same wake-up (§3.6 round 3). Correlation (G1) is therefore the arm's
  work at tier S and is graded through consequences (§5): a mis-routed event leaves its true case's required effects
  missing and puts unexpected effects on the wrong one.
- `world` channel: world-level events go to the next sweep tick of the sweep that spec-E names for them.
- Duplicates (W1), out-of-order (W2) and late events (W3) are delivered as the world generates them; the host never
  filters them.

Events carry raw business keys as the counterparty wrote them (possibly mistyped). Edition files: default variants
deliver `edition.published` with the file; sealed no-notice variants make the file appear in `rulebook/` at its
publication time with no event, or not at all (E-BED §2.5).

### 3.3 Effects, keys and the ledger

- The host applies a tick's effects **in output order** through typed mocks, appending one ledger row per application:
  `{ world, arm, instance, at, system, op, key, argsDigest, subjectCase, committed, faultTags }`.
- `schemas.effects[op].keyed` marks ops that take an idempotency key. A keyed op whose key is already committed on that
  system returns the original result and is **not applied again**; the same key with different arguments is rejected
  (`409 key-conflict`, not applied). An un-keyed op is applied every time it is emitted.
- `status(key)` exists on every keyed system, is read-only and always allowed: `committed | not-found | pending`.
- `after: key` defers an effect until the named effect of the same output has committed; if that one fails, the
  deferred effect is not applied and its result is `error` (`predecessor-failed`). `compensates: key` records intent
  (journaled; the oracle grades compensation by the effects that happen, not by this field).
- Results come back as `effect.result` events `{ key, system, op, status: ok | error | unknown | rate-limited |
  partial, retryAfter?, items?, error? }` on the instance channel at `at + latency`, latency ≥ 1 minute as declared per
  system in spec-E, so no result lands in the instant that produced it.
- Read-only ops (`status`, `*.get`, `sor.query`) never enter the effect ledger and are never graded.

### 3.4 State: SoR, carry, obligations

- **Business records** are written only by effects on systems of record. `sor/<instance>.json` holds the records the
  instance's effects created or touched plus those named by its subject; everything else is reachable by `sor.query`.
- **Product-computed fields** (declared `computedBy: 'product'` in `schemas.sor`): in Track P the arm computes and
  writes them through effects, and they are never copied from the reference into any input; in Track C the host's
  product engine computes them (§7).
- **Carry** is opaque to the host, committed with a compare-and-swap on the instance's tick index (one writer per
  instance). If a case-tick delivers no valid output, the previous carry is kept and a `carry-lost` row is journaled
  (DESIGN-STREAMS §2.3 [M]).
- **Obligations** are wake-ups only. The host fires each at `dueAt` as `timer.fired { obligationId, onDue }` on the
  instance channel. `dueAt <= now` is fired at `now + 1 minute` and logged. The host never computes `dueAt`.


# The arm interface (excerpt of the host README)

**Arm** (`{ id, tick(input, ctx), sweep?(input, ctx), workspace?: { dir } }`). `input` is the contract's CaseTickInput or
SweepTickInput. `ctx` gives `files` (`spec/SPEC-E.md`, `master/`, `calendar/`, `rulebook/<edition>/…` for published
editions only, `api/<system>.schema.json` with any active F5 rename, `sor/<instance>.json`, `docs/<eventId>.txt`),
`query('system.table', { where, limit })`, `status(system, key)`, `instances({ subject })` (H-1), `workspace` and
`recordCost(row)` (rows from the model journal; never solver reports). Every read is as of the round start. A `ctx` lives
only while its call runs: once the call returns, throws or times out, every method throws (H-26), so an adapter must
record a call's costs before the call settles.
----
// Parent orchestrator: routing and the order of the phases (DECOMPOSITION.md §3). It emits no effects itself: every
// system is reached through a module. Owns SPEC-E §1 (routing, subject, delivery order), §3.2, §3.5, §4 preamble,
// R7.1 (no close while a module is busy), and the phase order of S5, S8-S13, S15.
//
// Parent state (JSON; the whole carry stays under 64 KB):
//   { phase: 'new'|'intake'|'fraud'|'waiting'|'processing'|'ended', orderId, placedAt, customerId, currency, edition,
//     fulfilments: [{ fulfilmentId, location, available, items, amountMinor }], readyAt, readyDone,
//     started: { [fid]: true }, sagas: { [fid]: { end, reported } }, busy: { [module]: boolean }, held: [action] }

const FULFILMENT_ENDS = ['completed', 'failed'];

export function tick(input, ctx) {
  const state = input.state ?? newState();
  const events = input.events ?? [];
  let first = true;
  if (state.phase === 'new') {
    const r = firstTick(events[0], state, input, ctx);
    if (r.merge) return { decisions: { subject: r.subject }, state, merge: { into: r.merge } };
    if (r.close) return { decisions: { subject: r.subject ?? null }, state, close: true };
    first = false;
  }
  for (const ev of first ? events : events.slice(1)) handleEvent(ev, state, input, ctx);
  if (state.phase === 'processing') endOfTick(state, input, ctx);
  const out = { decisions: { subject: state.orderId ?? null }, state };
  if (shouldClose(state)) out.close = true;
  return out;
}

function newState() {
  return { phase: 'new', orderId: null, placedAt: null, customerId: null, currency: null, edition: null, fulfilments: [],
    readyAt: null, readyDone: false, started: {}, sagas: {}, busy: {}, held: [] };
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The first tick of an instance started by a message (§1 Routing, §3.2, §3.3, §3.5)

function firstTick(ev, state, input, ctx) {
  if (!ev) return { close: true, subject: null };
  if (ev.type === 'migration') return resumeMigration(ev, state, input, ctx);
  if (ev.type === 'order.placed') {
    const orderId = normalise(ev.payload.orderId, ctx);
    const open = openElsewhere(orderId, input, ctx);
    if (open) return { merge: open, subject: orderId };
    if (recordedOrder(orderId, ctx)) return { close: true, subject: orderId };     // R1.4: a recorded duplicate
    state.orderId = orderId;
    state.placedAt = ev.at;                                                        // §3.1: placedAt is the event's time
    state.customerId = ev.payload.customerId;
    state.currency = ev.payload.currency;
    state.phase = 'intake';
    const r = ctx.call('intake', orderId, { op: 'placed', order: placedOrder(ev, orderId) });
    absorbIntake(r, state, ctx);
    return { subject: orderId };
  }
  if (ev.type === 'customer.action') {
    const orderId = normalise(ev.payload.orderRef, ctx);
    if (!orderId) return { close: true, subject: null };                          // §3.2: names no order: nothing
    const open = openElsewhere(orderId, input, ctx);
    if (open) return { merge: open, subject: orderId };
    return { close: true, subject: orderId };                                     // ended or unknown: changes nothing
  }
  if (ev.type === 'carrier.status') {
    const orderId = orderOfFulfilment(ev.payload.fulfilmentRef, ctx);
    if (orderId) {
      const open = openElsewhere(orderId, input, ctx);
      if (open) return { merge: open, subject: orderId };
    }
    return { close: true, subject: orderId };                                    // §3.3: changes nothing
  }
  return { close: true, subject: null };
}

function resumeMigration(ev, state, input, ctx) {
  // §3.5: continue from where the order stands. The records say what exists; the messages already received are
  // replayed through the same handlers (they are idempotent), and the rules continue unchanged.
  const rec = ev.payload.records ?? {};
  const row = (rec['orders.orders'] ?? [])[0];
  if (!row) return { close: true, subject: null };
  const orderId = normalise(row.orderId, ctx);
  const rows = rec['orders.fulfilments'] ?? [];
  state.orderId = orderId;
  state.customerId = row.customerId;
  state.currency = row.currency;
  state.placedAt = row.placedAt;
  state.edition = ctx.call('intake', orderId, { op: 'resume', records: rec }).edition;
  state.fulfilments = rows.map(f => ({ fulfilmentId: f.fulfilmentId, location: f.location ?? null, available: (f.location ?? null) !== null,
    items: mergeItems(f.items ?? []), amountMinor: f.amountMinor ?? 0 }));
  ctx.call('statuses', orderId, { op: 'resume', orderId, records: { order: row.status, fulfilments: rows.map(f => ({ fulfilmentId: f.fulfilmentId, status: f.status })) } });
  ctx.call('notices', orderId, { op: 'resume', customerId: row.customerId, sent: (rec['notify.notifications'] ?? []).map(n => `${n.template}.${n.about}`) });
  const pending = row.status === 'pending';
  if (pending) {
    // Still before clearing: the fraud check is read again; a review task is asked again if the verdict needs one.
    ctx.call('fraud', orderId, { op: 'start', orderId, placedAt: row.placedAt, edition: state.edition });
    state.phase = 'fraud';
  } else {
    ctx.call('fraud', orderId, { op: 'resume', records: { state: 'cleared', placedAt: row.placedAt, edition: state.edition, at: row.placedAt } });
    state.phase = row.status === 'processing' ? 'processing' : row.status === 'customerActionRequired' ? 'waiting' : 'ended';
  }
  const ca = ctx.call('customer-action', orderId, { op: 'resume', placedAt: row.placedAt, edition: state.edition, records: rec });
  state.busy.ca = ca.busy;
  if (state.phase === 'processing') {
    state.readyAt = row.placedAt;
    state.readyDone = true;
    ctx.call('order-progress', orderId, { op: 'resume', records: { state: 'processing' } });
    for (const f of state.fulfilments) {
      if (!f.available) continue;
      state.started[f.fulfilmentId] = true;
      const out = ctx.call('fulfilment', f.fulfilmentId, { op: 'resume', records: fulfilmentRecord(f, state, rec, rows) });
      onFulfilment(f.fulfilmentId, out, state, ctx);
    }
  }
  for (const e of ev.payload.events ?? []) if (e.type !== 'order.placed') handleEvent(e, state, input, ctx);
  return { subject: orderId };
}

// The fulfilment's saga state, read back from its records (reservations, charge, shipment and status rows).
function fulfilmentRecord(f, state, rec, rows) {
  const fid = f.fulfilmentId;
  const status = (rows.find(r => r.fulfilmentId === fid) ?? {}).status ?? 'pending';
  const reserved = (rec['inventory.reservations'] ?? []).filter(x => x.fulfilmentId === fid && x.status === 'active').map(x => x.sku);
  const charged = (rec['billing.charges'] ?? []).some(x => x.fulfilmentId === fid);
  const shipment = (rec['carrier.shipments'] ?? []).find(x => x.fulfilmentId === fid);
  const shipped = shipment !== undefined && shipment.status !== 'cancelled' && shipment.status !== 'failed';
  // Reports that already came are read back from the shipment: an earlier time, so they count for S7 (assumption).
  const reports = shipment && ['collected', 'inTransit', 'delivered'].includes(shipment.status)
    ? [{ meaning: shipment.status, reportedAt: '1970-01-01T00:00:00+00:00' }] : [];
  const phase = { pending: 'running', processing: 'running', dispatched: 'dispatched', completed: 'completed', failed: 'failed', cancelled: 'stopped' }[status] ?? 'running';
  const end = { completed: 'completed', failed: 'failed', cancelled: 'stopped' }[status] ?? null;
  return {
    orderId: state.orderId, fid, customerId: state.customerId, currency: state.currency, edition: state.edition,
    location: f.location, items: f.items, amountMinor: f.amountMinor, phase, batches: 0,
    reserve: Object.fromEntries(f.items.map(i => [i.sku, reserved.includes(i.sku) ? 'ok' : 'none'])),
    charge: charged ? 'ok' : 'none', ship: shipped ? 'ok' : 'none', shipUndo: 'none', refund: 'none',
    collectionFailed: shipment !== undefined && shipment.status === 'failed', dispatched: status === 'dispatched' || status === 'completed',
    reports, end, endedAt: null,
  };
}

function mergeItems(items) {
  const bySku = new Map();
  for (const i of items) bySku.set(i.sku, (bySku.get(i.sku) ?? 0) + i.qty);
  return [...bySku].map(([sku, qty]) => ({ sku, qty }));
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Each event of the tick, in the order given (§1 Delivery order; never re-sorted)

function handleEvent(ev, state, input, ctx) {
  if (ev.type === 'effect.result' || ev.type === 'timer.fired' || ev.type.startsWith('humanTask.')) {
    const owner = ctx.ownerOf(ev);
    if (!owner) return;
    const out = ctx.deliver(ev);
    onModuleOutput(owner.module, owner.key, out, state, ctx);
    return;
  }
  if (ev.type === 'order.placed') {
    // A merged duplicate of a placed order (R1.4): intake recognises it.
    const r = ctx.call('intake', state.orderId, { op: 'placed', order: placedOrder(ev, state.orderId) });
    absorbIntake(r, state, ctx);
    return;
  }
  if (ev.type === 'customer.action') return routeCustomerAction(ev, state, ctx, normalise(ev.payload.orderRef, ctx));
  if (ev.type === 'carrier.status') {
    const fid = ev.payload.fulfilmentRef;
    if (!state.fulfilments.some(f => f.fulfilmentId === fid)) return;             // §3.3: unknown fulfilment: nothing
    const out = ctx.call('fulfilment', fid, { op: 'carrier', message: ev.payload, at: ev.at });
    onModuleOutput('fulfilment', fid, out, state, ctx);
  }
}

function routeCustomerAction(ev, state, ctx, orderId) {
  const at = ev.at;
  const action = ev.payload.action;
  if (orderId !== state.orderId) return;
  const cancelOf = () => ({ orderId, at, eventId: ev.id, fulfilmentIds: state.fulfilments.filter(f => f.available).map(f => f.fulfilmentId) });
  switch (state.phase) {
    case 'intake':
      // Recorded later: held and routed when the order is recorded (open point, DECOMPOSITION.md §8).
